WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:05.660
Once upon a time, American actor and martial
artist Wesley Snipes was the most in-demand

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.000
action star in show business—and for good
reason.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.610
Snipes' unique ability to be both athletic
and charming led to some very successful performances.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:18.040
These days, however, Hollywood doesn't seem
to give him much work anymore, and here's

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:18.560
why.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.040
Hitting it big

00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:24.300
Wesley Snipes' career got off to a blazing
start when he landed the role of Willie Mays

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.679
Hayes, the swift-footed base-stealer in the
baseball-comedy classic Major League.

00:00:28.679 --> 00:00:32.940
But while that showcase of quick wit might've
been a home run with casting directors, he

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.070
let his overnight fame get to his head a little
too quickly.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:39.910
"I plan to put on a hitting display."

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:43.950
Snipes reportedly turned down the chance to
star in the sequel, Major League II, which

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.050
ticked off some of his co-stars.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.190
Corbin Bernsen, for one, said of the diss,

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:50.960
"I said, 'Hey, man, they're gonna make Major
League II!'

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.030
And he was like, 'You're gonna do that?'

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:55.700
And I thought, 'Wow, how quickly they forget.'

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.700
He'd become Wesley Snipes.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.200
That rubbed me the wrong way."

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.240
Although Snipes had earned the ire of his
Major League team, he did earn quite a few

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.450
grand slams right out of the dugout, including
Jungle Fever, White Men Can't Jump, Passenger

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:12.549
57, Demolition Man, and Murder at 1600.

00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:16.340
But while he was never a critical darling,
despite his box office prowess, his career

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.670
started to cool once a pattern of legal problems
began to emerge.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.420
Facing handcuffs

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.829
Snipes' first brush with the law wasn't his
fault.

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:29.509
In 1991, he was arrested while driving a car
leased by his production company and claimed

00:01:29.509 --> 00:01:34.100
he was assaulted and humiliated by the police
as a result of racial profiling.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:39.389
But Snipes' second major run-in with the law
was brought on, this time, by legitimate reasons—and

00:01:39.389 --> 00:01:42.270
would put a dent in the actor's professional
reputation.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:47.869
In August 1993, Snipes was cited for carrying
a concealed weapon loaded with half a dozen

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:53.119
rounds of hollow point bullets, after a fender-bending
motorcycle accident in Hollywood.

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:58.319
Snipes received two years of unsupervised
probation and was fined $2,700.

00:01:58.319 --> 00:02:02.850
The following year, Snipes' reputation as
a law-abiding citizen decreased even further

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:08.259
when the actor led a Florida state trooper
on a 120 mile-per-hour high-speed chase, which

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:11.569
ended in the action star crashing his motorcycle.

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:15.821
Snipes got off with a citation for reckless
driving and refused medical treatment, but

00:02:15.821 --> 00:02:20.330
his reputation was immediately damaged by
the unusual incident.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:21.530
Bouncing back

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:26.129
Just when it seemed like Snipes' run-ins with
the law and string of less-than-stellar performances

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:30.580
were on the brink of completely bankrupting
his career, the action star bounced back with

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:36.349
his most iconic role yet: the title vampire-slaying
superhero in Blade.

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:41.260
Despite only receiving mediocre critical reviews,
the film was a massive success, spawning a

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:46.939
pair of sequels and cementing Snipes as both
a household name and bona fide action hero.

00:02:46.939 --> 00:02:50.760
After the commercial success of Blade, Snipes's
career was on the upswing again.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.160
Unfortunately for him, that second rise was
followed by an even bigger fall.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.160
Falling again

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.530
After Blade, the actor starred in a string
of films that failed to court the same kind

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.010
of audience as his earlier pics.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:08.930
First up was The Art of War, which was a massive
failure, both critically and commercially.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:12.989
Then there was ZigZag and Undisputed, which
dug the hole even deeper.

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:16.549
The final nail in the coffin for Snipes was
Blade: Trinity.

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:21.680
In addition to being the least-liked film
in the series, it all but ensured major studios

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.940
would never work with Snipes again—especially
after Snipes filed a lawsuit against New Line

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.689
Cinema and the filmmakers to the tune of $5
million.

00:03:30.689 --> 00:03:35.159
Snipes alleged the filmmakers and supporting
cast were all approved without his express

00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:37.599
consent, which violated his contract.

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:43.450
He also claimed writer-director David Goyer
made racist remarks regarding Snipes' professionalism

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.819
and refused to punish a crew member who wore
a racist T-shirt while filming.

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:53.349
On top of that, Snipes claimed New Line Cinema
intentionally hired only white individuals,

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:58.299
still owed him $3 million, and didn't help
the actor obtain a tax exemption.

00:03:58.299 --> 00:04:03.239
Co-star Patton Oswalt, however, claimed that
Snipes was a problem during production.

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:08.209
"And Wesley Snipes was going crazy, and he
wouldn't come out of his trailer…"

00:04:08.209 --> 00:04:11.060
"He would only answer to the name Blade."

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:15.189
After that, Oswalt said, Snipes attempted
to force director David Goyer to resign from

00:04:15.189 --> 00:04:16.189
the film.

00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:19.539
But his biggest problems were only beginning
to take shape.

00:04:19.539 --> 00:04:21.340
Uncle Sam slam

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.130
Perhaps the most notorious reas on Wesley
Snipes' career soured is that he was convicted

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:31.259
on three misdemeanor counts for failing to
file three years' worth of tax returns, which

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:34.639
apparently cheated Uncle Sam out of $7 million.

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:39.289
The prosecution blamed Snipes for following
the advice of both his accountant and another

00:04:39.289 --> 00:04:41.130
firm anti-tax proponent.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:45.979
Snipes, who had already paid millions of dollars
in taxes, was sentenced to three years in

00:04:45.979 --> 00:04:50.800
prison in 2008, before being transferred to
the New York Community Corrections Office

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:51.800
for home confinement.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:57.430
As soon as Snipes got out of prison, the IRS
piled on the civil tax collections, arguing

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.759
that the actor owed them close to $18 million.

00:05:00.759 --> 00:05:05.889
Snipes decided he had enough, and filed a
lawsuit against the government's tax collection

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:06.889
agency.

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:11.270
Snipes argued that the IRS was just arbitrarily
throwing out figures in an effort to collect

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.530
as much money as possible.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:14.800
Time for a turnaround?

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.880
Though Snipes doesn't get very many big-time
movie offers anymore, he still gets plenty

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.740
of small-time movie offers.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:25.090
Most recently, he starred in 2017's Armed
Response and The Recall.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:30.190
He also had a role in The Expendables 3—which
served to put Snipes' name back into a big-budget

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:31.190
film.

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:36.370
Even more promising, Snipes played Cyclops
in Spike Lee's Chi-Raq which is undoubtedly

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:39.440
the best project to involve the actor in recent
decades.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.370
"Okay, okay, them 30 poppers?"

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:46.380
He's also shown a new interest in dancing
and musical theater lately.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:50.680
"Doing movies was cool but it still wasn't
fulfilling what I wanted to do—even to this

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:51.500
day.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.720
If I have a choice between doing a movie or
doing a dance show, I'll do the dance show

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:55.720
first."

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.780
On top of that, he's also been busy writing
what he hopes will ultimately be a trilogy

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:04.860
of books, the first of which—Talon of God—is
on shelves now.

00:06:04.879 --> 00:06:08.090
All thing considered, it's not the time to
write off Wesley Snipes.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:09.090
Not yet.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:10.310
Thanks for watching!

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:12.800
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.271
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

